#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "579373bc-38b3-584c-a118-84be7dfc9074" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing the Relative Magnitude of a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d37335aa-7471-5264-af40-7d613aec7144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280052} {:answer/id #uuid "b7cb8538-1520-5df2-ad95-a6d576b39880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280053} {:answer/id #uuid "4990051a-bb0d-551c-a7b8-9e34f9335163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280054} {:answer/id #uuid "617e94d7-3420-5268-a870-3c941eb5d2c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280055} {:answer/id #uuid "47add63a-8f81-55bf-8120-bd13a27e45e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "617e94d7-3420-5268-a870-3c941eb5d2c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280055} :answer-field/id #uuid "a95398ac-5607-570e-b4c8-81e654018c20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5784fc5-be1c-559b-a5ef-47d7ce89d4ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-106830" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x-3}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $2x-3$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x-3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/assets/ab3f7928be50c25689a99751cd180e585d749bf0a5c253c8662f9b820bbf4bb7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "579373bc-38b3-584c-a118-84be7dfc9074" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing the Relative Magnitude of a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "491f86fd-1b83-5929-9ea3-d1af2b627487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "491f86fd-1b83-5929-9ea3-d1af2b627487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280059} :answer-field/id #uuid "82a4dafa-74b6-5c92-b328-33a58173a9f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19e3e79b-7ea0-51f4-a23f-f7c4a33f8e78" :question/math-academy-id "q-338817" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/assets/e164233abcdf9062fd856a100fb7b792912fa030746a63f01ca7fd6683bf7f09.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5c89e90-7c0c-55bb-a9fe-d0a5bd03160a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280062} {:answer/id #uuid "21e3b8d0-db29-5c36-bb56-9d5eaf68f058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280063} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d5eb73-1d1a-50ae-a433-6d46b4df4a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280064} {:answer/id #uuid "84880119-8cc6-5cae-949f-5b88ff8298aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280065} {:answer/id #uuid "e44d4e63-9064-5e3b-a606-e35ac871964f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5c89e90-7c0c-55bb-a9fe-d0a5bd03160a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280062} :answer-field/id #uuid "31582fc7-6e6c-540d-b682-5e28f452fca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28924c15-bb63-5eb8-a04e-6f49ec2c95fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-68105" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+3).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+3) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}+3) \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "433ac237-32ff-591c-ab0d-e271f5beda30" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Polynomial Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb9977a-6a3b-57af-8d4c-1e2320bfec74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280069} {:answer/id #uuid "0a3afe49-1eab-5b89-822b-91455a0d1c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280070} {:answer/id #uuid "e908e853-0aaf-5674-a657-5e745d892ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280071} {:answer/id #uuid "da0ba03b-c448-5fc4-8aa2-d4b5331a39e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280072} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ac4557-9ae1-5e49-bc78-ed1b9b80de18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a3afe49-1eab-5b89-822b-91455a0d1c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280070} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9339596-beda-5277-8b44-a4556a79a05a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74924b73-40df-5d43-a6ea-180964065ee8" :question/math-academy-id "q-78026" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)}+6)=$" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{5}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)}=\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)}+6)=\\infty \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c538b0a9-a084-5a83-a5fa-eca4e0270a93" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing an Exponential Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21dff4da-152c-51f0-8f60-2665be9c5493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280076} {:answer/id #uuid "0a01b433-f5cd-54f3-b145-764324fa1f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280077} {:answer/id #uuid "98a78a0e-f1bd-59d2-b190-3846e6726382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280078} {:answer/id #uuid "b66bac71-460c-5348-8278-8e120599f52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280079} {:answer/id #uuid "efd04212-5f84-562e-9556-b9baa4bbe8d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b66bac71-460c-5348-8278-8e120599f52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280079} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ace30ae-7a0b-5c03-a519-6ffb17f90ac6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2593ec58-15ba-5bdf-a694-27d214610ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-78035" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
